Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  CorrectedType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  17 REMOVED_MONTH 03 ACCESSIONED ON  18 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 09 17 CLINICAL DATA  History of invasive ductal carcinoma in  REMOVED_CASE_ID   grade III III  with dermal and epidermal involvement                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY  ANGIOSARCOMA  HIGH GRADE  GRADE REMOVED_DATE OF 3   EXTENDING THROUGHOUT THE ENTIRE SPECIMEN  17 CM   AND BROADLY PRESENT AT THE POSTERIOR AND SKIN MARGINS  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE REMOVED_DATE OF 3  PRESENT WITHIN AN AREA OF DENSE SCLEROSIS CONSISTENT WITH SITE OF TREATED TUMOR  AND EXTENDING TO THE POSTERIOR MARGIN  ONE LYMPH NODE WITH NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The angiosarcoma may have arisen from the invasive ductal carcinoma and thus represent a component of metaplastic carcinoma  or it may possibly be a postirradiation sarcoma  Although the angiosarcoma and invasive ductal carcinoma are juxtaposed in several areas  clear merging of the two tumor types is not identified  The angiosarcoma diffusely involves the skin and focally invades the deep skeletal muscle  The angiosarcoma tumor cells are diffusely positive for factor VIII   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and vimentin  and are focally positive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   They are negative for cytokeratins  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  3 Cam5 2  wide spectrum  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   as well as S 100  desmin  and ulex  These immunohistochemical findings support the diagnosis of angiosarcoma  Slides of the patient s prior biopsy   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   are not currently available for comparison  Receptor studies will be reported in an addendum  Diagnosis by  MELINDA J  FAN  MD Signed On  26 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 11 52                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block C   The invasive ductal carcinoma tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The invasive ductal carcinoma tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  The invasive ductal carcinoma tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  Breast  Left  block J   Rare angiosarcoma tumor cells stain faintly for estrogen receptor protein  Rare angiosarcoma tumor cells stain faintly for progesterone receptor protein  The angiosarcoma tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Atul Kumar Bhan  M D  Signed On  02 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 16 28     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     THIS ADDENDUM HAS BEEN DELETED FOR THIS REPORT GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast   is a 17 x 14 x 9cm simple mastectomy specimen  The entire anterior surface is covered by skin  which is notable for numerous fungating  necrotic tan red and green brown polypoid masses which cover nearly 50  of the surface  The radial skin resection margins are grossly positive for tumor in several areas  The posterior resection margin consists of adipose tissue and skeletal tissue  The resection margins  all posterior  are inked black  and the specimen is serially sectioned  The dermis and underlying parenchyma is diffusely thickened by tumor throughout the entire breast  Deeper in the tissue is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm firm fibrotic region that grossly abuts the attached skeletal muscles  Additional nodules of tumor are scattered throughout the breast parenchyma  Tumor is grossly present at the deep margin  Representative sections are submitted as follows  A D  Fibrotic mass to deep resection margin  E F  Skin  G H  Other masses in parenchyma  I J  Skin margins  en face  K  Tumor to skeletal muscle  L  Tumor to deep margin  Also received in this case  is a nonoriented piece of lobulated yellow grossly hemorrhagic adipose tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm  The external surface is entirely inked black and the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a heterogeneous brown red white ill defined cut surface  A representative section is submitted in cassette K  Resident  JOSHUA B  KISH  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Cameron Dorrans Wright  M D  William Gerald Austen  M D  PART   BREAST LEFT  SIMPLE MASTECTOMY , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  16 AUG 93 ACCESSIONED ON  16 AUG 93 at 15 21 CLINICAL DATA  Right mammographic abnormality                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2 OF 3  85   AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  15    NOTE  The carcinoma measures 0 7cm in greatest dimension  No lymphatic or vascular invasion is seen  Intraductal carcinoma is present at the margin  Diagnosis by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D  Signed On  18 AUG 93 at 17 24                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  25 AUG 93 at 09 13 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Right breast mass  is a REMOVED_DATE x 3 x 1 4cm piece of fibrofatty tissue with a wire  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a white firm nodule measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm with ill defined borders grossly within 0 1cm of one inked margin  The lesion was too small to freeze  The lesion is submitted in A and B  The remainder of the specimen is submitted in C through G  Resident  Lisa H  Lerner  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS  RIGHT BREAST TISSUE   SMALL SUSPICIOUS AREA  NOT LARGE ENOUGH TO FREEZE  O R  Resident  James Kirby  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Christophe Girardet  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Barbara Lynn Smith  M D  PART   RIGHT BREAST TISSUE , Unchecked.
